5 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049882 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049882 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049882 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428427 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049882 #edb/ref 1031 "Level Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049882 #edb/ref 1033 1900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049882 #edb/ref 1034 1.3150851958569647 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049882 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "1ad374f3-e4f4-5dcc-ab6d-d6aed0790f3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050045 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050045 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050045 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050045 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050045 #edb/ref 1031 "Level Surfaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050045 #edb/ref 1033 1978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050045 #edb/ref 1034 0.7782992683918253 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050045 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "28837c1e-d226-56e8-b958-b4375cfa8078" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050612 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050612 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050612 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050612 #edb/ref 1031 "The Bivariate Normal Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050612 #edb/ref 1033 918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050612 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "58cd99c7-ea79-5204-8fe5-0ad9cfae548a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124287 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4228" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124287 #edb/ref 1071 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+2y^2-2}{x^2}$ are given by $f(x,y) = c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=1$ gives a circle\n\nII. $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n\nIII. $c=2$ gives a hyperbola" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124287 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5791bd77-96c7-571d-b8b5-76cb62eb0a9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124287 #edb/ref 1074 "Setting $f(x,y) = c$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2}{{x}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2 & =c{x}^{2} \\\\ (1-c){x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c = 1,$ then the level curve is $y^2 = 1,$ which consists of two lines.\n\n- If $c = 0,$ then the level curve is $x^2+2y^2=2,$ which is an ellipse.\n\n- If $c = 2,$ then the level curve is $2y^2-x^2=2$ which is a hyperbola.\n\nSo, only statements II and III are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124288 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124288 #edb/ref 1071 "Sketch the level curves of the surface $z=e^{x^2-y^2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124288 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "618e7097-38d3-5167-a9d2-5d5a3fc85e0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124288 #edb/ref 1074 "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)=e^{x^2-y^2}$ take the form\n\n$$\ne^{x^2-y^2} = c.\n$$\n\nNote that we must have the constraint $c\\gt 0$ since $e^x \\gt 0$ for all $x.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡c \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can split this into three cases, depending on the sign of $\\ln c.$\n\n- Case 1: $\\ln c \\gt 0.$ In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $x^2-y^2 = \\ln c.$\n\n- Case 2: $\\ln c = 0.$ In this case, we have the lines $y=\\pm x.$\n\n- Case 3: $\\ln c < 0.$ In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $y^2 - x^2 = -\\ln c.$\n\nTherefore, the level curves are as follows:\n\n![](images/5f/5f2ec43639cb31e03cc4a00e43969c7f0333c098fc4b366ea1609772faabfda5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124362 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5968" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124362 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ for the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{12y}{x^2-y^2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124362 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fe87d9e4-6445-5ee7-9dc7-6ab8fbb165c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124362 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $G$:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n  f\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)&= \\dfrac{12(1)}{\\big(\\sqrt{5}\\big)^2 - 1^2} = 3}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ is the $3$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{12y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ 4y & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4y-4 & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-({y}^{2}+4y+4) & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-(y+2)^{2} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ is the hyperbola $x^2 - (y+2)^2 = -4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124475 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124475 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124475 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124475 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124475 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124475 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124475 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124475 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124475 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124475 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124475 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124475 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124475 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592