 1083 #uuid "e7c9fa4a-b421-5b11-b4d3-07241c8d5153" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335537 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335537 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335537 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335537 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335537 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186335011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335537 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335537 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335537 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186335011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335537 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9fbd657b-a196-55b7-85e6-cbcb244ec565" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335762 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335762 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335762 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335762 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)={e}^{xy}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335762 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6cc7e8a4-946a-52ee-b5f1-ac0fda396a47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335762 #edb/ref 1074 "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nNotice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $(0,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider only level curves for $c>0.$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ {e}^{xy} & =c \\\\ xy & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡c \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡c}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can split this into three cases, depending on the sign of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.$\n\n- Case 1: $\\operatorname{ln}⁡c>0.$ In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡c}{x}.$\n\n- Case 2: $\\operatorname{ln}⁡c=0.$ In this case, we have the lines $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Case 3: $\\operatorname{ln}⁡c<0.$ In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $y=-\\frac{|\\operatorname{ln}⁡c|}{x}.$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas centered at the origin. Therefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/d3/d330ddb12d1df9d31af3d082b04271d02df381739064a6fe2203dabce358a09f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371735 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186471992 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186371735 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186471993 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186371735 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186471994 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186371735 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129059" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371735 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371735 #edb/ref 1071 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+y}{x}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $c=-1$ gives the $x$-axis, excluding the origin\n- **II.** $c=0$ gives the $y$-axis, excluding the origin\n- **III.** $c=1$ gives the line $y=x,$ excluding the origin\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186371735 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac11e3e9-cb1c-5541-98a0-caa6e8b5b278" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371735 #edb/ref 1074 "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{x} & =c \\\\ x+y & =cx \\\\ y & =(c-1)x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=-1,$ the level curve is the line $y=-2x,$ excluding the origin. So, I is false.\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is the line $y=-x,$ excluding the origin. So, II is false.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is $y=0,$ which is the $x$-axis (excluding the origin). So, III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the listed\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373132 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472241 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186373132 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472242 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186373132 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472243 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186373132 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71402" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373132 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373132 #edb/ref 1071 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=9x+3y-4$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n- **II.** $c=-4$ gives a line\n- **III.** $c=5$ gives a hyperbola" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186373132 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c853fbcf-5be0-5101-8f2a-558cbd4a782f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373132 #edb/ref 1074 "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x+3y-4 & =c \\\\ 3y & =-9x+c+4 \\\\ y & =-3x+\\frac{c+4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curve is $y=-3x+\\frac{4}{3},$ which is not an ellipse.\n\n- If $c=-4$ then the level curve is $y=-3x,$ which is a line.\n\n- If $c=5$ then the level curve is $y=-3x+3,$ which is not a hyperbola.\n\nSo, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378552 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186378553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378552 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128445" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378552 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378552 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(1,0)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378552 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "adad43ad-f556-54b5-872d-89fc29158b9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378552 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+0+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(1,0)$ is the $\\frac{1}{2}$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(1,0)$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378553 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378553 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378553 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378553 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378553 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378553 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378553 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378553 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186378556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378553 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9f45af6a-ee19-5560-9c17-22c9bcaae7c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378554 #edb/ref 1080 "2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378554 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378554 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a54c10a5-9ef4-586a-8955-c8ef90f8bd0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378555 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378555 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378555 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3e62eedc-e697-557e-a7eb-17e16f14ea94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378556 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378556 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378556 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9b7ce305-e6ff-5ce3-8a7a-084edafec71e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378557 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378557 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378557 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1b55e37c-193c-5283-9221-71aafd335783" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378558 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378558 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378558 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14db4b6d-e265-54a1-97c8-5f4f9c459c7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379889 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186379890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379889 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71193" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379889 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379889 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $T(2,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x-{y}^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379889 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae2ee110-6028-560b-b154-cea2add1f458" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379889 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $T$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,1) & =\\frac{1}{2-{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $T(2,1)$ is the $1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{1}{x-{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ 1 & =x-{y}^{2} \\\\ x & =1+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $T(2,1)$ is the parabola $x=1+{y}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379890 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379890 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379890 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186379891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379890 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5829d29b-d41b-548e-a76a-f71d52e87bf5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379891 #edb/ref 1080 "x=1+{y}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379891 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379891 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b1fe7f12-1aba-53b8-84a6-c65a5b3db079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379892 #edb/ref 1080 "y=2+{x}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379892 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379892 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "757f29e0-0793-5816-a02a-c5609b14f555" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379893 #edb/ref 1080 "y=1+{x}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379893 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379893 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03d173bd-9735-589b-ae2b-0f0a570f3cc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379894 #edb/ref 1080 "x=1+2{y}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379894 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379894 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2e380ecf-c597-530c-bacd-d038a0e62d9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379895 #edb/ref 1080 "x=2-{y}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379895 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379895 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3eeebbe3-4754-5082-87d4-a82c718991e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379903 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472235 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186379903 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472236 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186379903 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472237 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186379903 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71398" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379903 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379903 #edb/ref 1071 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2y}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $c=0$ gives a circle\n- **II.** $c=1$ gives a hyperbola\n- **III.** $c=2$ gives a parabola" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186379903 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d3aaf864-2fae-5bdc-a851-6e2a3303364c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379903 #edb/ref 1074 "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2y} & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+2y & ={c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curve is $y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},$ which is not a circle.\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is $y=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},$ which is not a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curve is $y=2-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},$ which is a parabola.\n\nSo, only statement III is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379911 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379911 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379911 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379911 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379911 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379911 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379911 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379911 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186379913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379911 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "15955093-e5c1-50f6-b2be-d700cd4f9e99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379912 #edb/ref 1080 "images/65/6521da99a82e38a68a5aa87a0c7c88eacf4ed7b78351af594ec309fa2cb7f57b.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379912 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379912 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8f17d518-60b8-544c-92f5-2cf0aab0557b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379913 #edb/ref 1080 "images/cc/cccaa6d7f554076b3a79364e1f2c4c061ae7cd86f7f0064e3861ba3b035b6a3e.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379913 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379913 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6196a8f6-ab71-5465-a24f-af55f550e44b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [